#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198032 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/id #uuid "5ff4ed47-dffe-534e-baf9-41bded65d457" :question/math-academy-id "e-12385" :question/problem "![](images/bd/bd504e1d8d85a2f2bbb4b75bd6825120a6aef15345c704714931b6270f84796f.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0, \\: 0, \\: 1 + xyz \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $\\mathbf F =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 1+xyz\\rangle$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/97/977d93ad65c9f3690def4242f98f4de5e5c89faf02fe47d06f5ed251d8a4a292.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 1$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 1$ on $S_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xyz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xy⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =1+xy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}1+xy\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}1+xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}1+xy\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[x+\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}y]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+\\frac{1}{2}y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+\\frac{1}{4}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $0 \\leq x \\leq 1$ and $0 \\leq y \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_2$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_2 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, -1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 0$ on $S_2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1+xyz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{2} & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-1\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-1\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-\\text{Area}(D) \\\\  & =-(1-0)(1-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}+(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198033 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "06cccfa3-4b70-5e67-9e9f-9f5c83b319bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-12386" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 2x,\\: 2y+xz,\\: 2z-xy\\rangle$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\dfrac{\\langle x, y, z\\rangle}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n = \\langle x, y, z\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x^2+y^2+z^2=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨2x,\\,2y+xz,\\,2z-xy⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+(2y+xz)\\cdot y+(2z-xy)\\cdot z \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}+2{y}^{2}+xyz+2{z}^{2}-xyz \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =8\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198046 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/id #uuid "8d046f27-886d-5342-8868-e2c3ae731fa5" :question/math-academy-id "e-12453" :question/problem "![](images/92/925b2f032f751e4482632c449fff35f544929f3355655c20d6701e640a7b20a3.png)\n\n \n\nConsider the vector field $\\mathbf F(x,y,z) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $\\mathbf n,$ and $\\mathbf F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $\\mathbf n$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S} =\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $\\mathbf F =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\:  z\\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/f5/f5a443b0bb234662c8f4a2bc156b458ac09a8db3ce3cc67933f7465a19db2ef9.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $\\mathbf F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let $S_1$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z = 4$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to $S_1$ is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf n_1 = \\langle 0,\\, 0,\\, 1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z = 4$ on $S_1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,z\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $\\mathbf F$ across $S_1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y) \\to (r,\\theta), \\textrm d A = r\\,\\textrm d r\\,\\textrm d \\theta,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}r\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}{r}^{2}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{3}{r}^{3}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =8\\pi (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let $S_2$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z = 0$ for $x^2 + y^2 \\leq 1.$ Therefore, for every point on $S_2,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0) = \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0 \\sqrt{x^2 + y^2} \\rangle =  \\langle 0,\\: 0,\\: 0\\rangle.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198601 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "310404f5-f868-54f7-b01b-a2a18674d442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275913} {:answer/id #uuid "92f872ee-006f-5878-8d2d-24cd0725ec4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276809} {:answer/id #uuid "ac8126eb-6bfa-51f0-a391-c2f103b6f6a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277027} {:answer/id #uuid "d62e99f6-a80a-5c52-82a2-08949d00886a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277362} {:answer/id #uuid "d9bb63e7-16d0-505d-be1f-49a3b1cdc699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277388}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "310404f5-f868-54f7-b01b-a2a18674d442" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275913} :answer-field/id #uuid "2af4217f-1686-5f6d-be11-0fc1f94ca999" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "300078ae-d2f8-55e0-bd83-9b0b9c3615b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-161162" :question/problem "![](images/89/8901e7155a476f0019b6d66e3b62b579b91daf99a448e497c93efce1d94415b0.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/8f/8fa71e16d84eb92506e9b10269182f907cb4f2437d0a9bdd3822da48c4cbba4a.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+2y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x+2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{1} & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+2y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{x}^{2}+2xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}1+2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[y+{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+yz⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{2} & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-2x\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}-2x\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}-2x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1\\cdot {[{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2+(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278424 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22aa9306-b821-5f7a-bcc1-13baa3e5f288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275787} {:answer/id #uuid "6643cf61-097c-51f6-b17b-d1aa26ebd1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276393} {:answer/id #uuid "86d98f42-d1f5-5fa9-b04c-8d2af7e4a2cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{27\\pi (4-\\sqrt{2})}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276689} {:answer/id #uuid "8a0b4ac1-ec6a-5fd6-aac1-0b33d91f5d3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi +3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276725} {:answer/id #uuid "ba1fe40a-9855-5b61-942f-7ed5bbdea556" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277126}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "22aa9306-b821-5f7a-bcc1-13baa3e5f288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275787} :answer-field/id #uuid "723b6093-df88-5ee2-b562-a30395cdbb69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f41018fb-b7c4-59de-93b6-b0fee9601c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161207" :question/problem "![](images/4c/4cb2c8128f24adddacd82b5f477848faa47ae52f95a5bb0636a8b286a6628a08.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/e6/e6fe01196298c1da0961fd01c73ee4cb7c71b6a03a027cceaba0e68be944c968.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{z}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,{2}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =4{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =4\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting $u=16-{r}^{2},$ we get $\\text{d}u=-2r\\,\\text{d}r.$ Also, the limits must be changed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & 0 & 2 \\\\ u & 16 & 12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these into our integral, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{2}r\\sqrt{16-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\int }_{16}^{12}\\sqrt{u}(-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u) \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{12}^{16}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{12}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}[64-24\\sqrt{3}] \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,{0}^{2}\\sqrt{16-{x}^{2}-{y}^{2}}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{64\\pi (8-3\\sqrt{3})}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08051b4d-e523-5380-b21a-12e9c0665d2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423370} {:answer/id #uuid "0ce314fb-b190-50fc-b3d0-c8253bbb95a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423373} {:answer/id #uuid "1a03aa61-a6a4-59bd-9047-ec009e7bb552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423376} {:answer/id #uuid "a7023940-c9dc-5a8b-a379-2f0028725950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423400} {:answer/id #uuid "f5a70d9b-245d-5412-9927-59ddc777a811" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423424}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a03aa61-a6a4-59bd-9047-ec009e7bb552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423376} :answer-field/id #uuid "919ef9fd-60f6-5c91-983a-1dea7afd83ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0aa0eff9-8245-5a13-9648-df63821931fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-161216" :question/problem "![](images/d3/d333ab14f8a42bb1d40894421afc76ff60acbb6c4cb858ddee5f6e8a39be2d15.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $1$ whose base is a circle of radius $2$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/51/5193451b39b03b11cf9780fd7039360ba8fb5de5876a3593f7a5dadde2e7be5b.png)\n\n \nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=1$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,y,0)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-1}⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-z}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\sqrt{1-0}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,y)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =-{∬}_{D}{r}^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-2\\pi \\cdot 4 \\\\  & =-8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0+(-8\\pi ) \\\\  & =-8\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e9c78dc-55fa-58d4-a093-f9544e5dfbd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275601} {:answer/id #uuid "78632b46-36da-5697-b93d-bc2adc1c9045" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276568} {:answer/id #uuid "79740243-a948-508f-ad95-f7961fb345fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276581} {:answer/id #uuid "7c58ab40-2a38-5ea8-a5ef-7548298d0ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276612} {:answer/id #uuid "7c6fd983-c520-5566-9c80-71e44ea0a60f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276613}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7c58ab40-2a38-5ea8-a5ef-7548298d0ad5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276612} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e7ff9e2-fb54-58a9-9896-204ca397e982" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df8892a4-0ff3-5c81-818d-9a1c55b02265" :question/math-academy-id "q-161219" :question/problem "![](images/9a/9afc7f79f4612ce579e82a6b3c5086922d14051978907bfeb5c133a3f99791c7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/fa/fa4986b1de44fa4ccc75114e64abfd93d0de16618f2f1b29695d9115019b6e3e.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=4$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =x{y}^{2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}+4\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}{y}^{2}+4x]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2{y}^{2}+8\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{2}{3}{y}^{3}+8y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le y\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}+z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,x{y}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-x{y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{1}{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y{\\int }_{0}^{2}x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{[\\frac{1}{3}{y}^{3}]}_{0}^{1}\\cdot {[\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{3}+(-\\frac{2}{3}) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423450 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06134337-3698-5e99-b304-3c524f8d58ba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423369} {:answer/id #uuid "b7b67ca6-6357-50a6-8510-6325cf38d5b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423402} {:answer/id #uuid "d03aaa3c-1379-5383-ba5a-20ab61ae0bbe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{19}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423412} {:answer/id #uuid "ddfc6802-b302-519c-b456-4ec4b85afbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423417} {:answer/id #uuid "e104162b-8025-5219-bed4-9b1e8c92a728" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423419}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddfc6802-b302-519c-b456-4ec4b85afbc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423417} :answer-field/id #uuid "723cf9c2-2b81-5164-9613-762892b1d534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423432}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b265113a-f521-5f72-933b-1132d7a6f831" :question/math-academy-id "q-161220" :question/problem "![](images/e8/e8708a16a0f6db347acf2c1a82a602d2fa8ce60d4bb8369f1414eca3465e4e71.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $z$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $z$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the top and bottom sides. \n\n![](images/8e/8ed6ffdeab54bc6888d245ef4f1c050c31e010c67dfa93aea365fc6cb523e382.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the top surface. On this surface, we have $z=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-4⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =2x+y-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+y-4\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{x}^{2}+xy-4x]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}y-3\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}-3y]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the bottom surface. On this surface, we have $z=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 1$ and $0\\le y\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $z=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y-2z⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,0,\\,2x+y⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,0,\\,-1⟩ \\\\  & =-(2x+y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(2x+y)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}2x+y\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}2x+y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{x}^{2}+xy]}_{x=0}^{x=1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =-{[y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{15}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{2}+(-\\frac{15}{2}) \\\\  & =-12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198606 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0382d2d7-f2e6-51ec-9901-b9a36c2fed17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275510} {:answer/id #uuid "2e0cfa47-c395-53a2-8163-e6e56df54b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275892} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0bf314-a120-5495-b1ec-846c190f9fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275993} {:answer/id #uuid "a35d6663-d50b-55ae-84c5-9f22edae8f1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276946} {:answer/id #uuid "c7e992d2-83f4-5097-82e2-b1717597091f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277251}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e0cfa47-c395-53a2-8163-e6e56df54b63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275892} :answer-field/id #uuid "d18a126a-d66c-5b81-b5fd-8257483e32f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278092}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9383b6ea-48e1-59a7-921c-a6c0f41ef499" :question/math-academy-id "q-161232" :question/problem "![](images/ea/ea320b4b8a036bdf905a51041eb94ffb2464125af912ec74830d49af922f4342.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x,\\,y+2z,\\,z-2y⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $\\sqrt{3}$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=\\sqrt{3}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=3$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x,\\,y+2z,\\,z-2y⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}⟨x,\\,y+2z,\\,z-2y⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}({x}^{2}+y(y+2z)+z(z-2y)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2yz+{z}^{2}-2yz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}\\sqrt{3}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot (\\sqrt{3})^{2} \\\\  & =12\\pi \\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278439 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "343581ff-9ea3-5966-b62d-24919144c694" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275940} {:answer/id #uuid "3e0609c2-f72d-540f-8a92-7731e9497bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276013} {:answer/id #uuid "c81831e6-499f-512e-a8c3-f443f0e9b327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277255} {:answer/id #uuid "df1ec40c-ff3a-5272-b18f-2110fffc08da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277429} {:answer/id #uuid "ee43dda4-5a54-5104-ba16-b4a58a588b1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c81831e6-499f-512e-a8c3-f443f0e9b327" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277255} :answer-field/id #uuid "70e58f9a-20a2-5473-91d0-c704034e220c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277927}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db773492-919e-574b-8b09-824e37e7dd1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-161237" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1ee9e32dc5dbe7ba1222a5448ce9ae1227a461a40a36f13e337fce7990263408.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨z,\\,0,\\,-x⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $4$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=4.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{4}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨z,\\,0,\\,-x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}⟨z,\\,0,\\,-x⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(xz+0-xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423451 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19ccd5aa-3240-5023-a265-5b3b645f65d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423375} {:answer/id #uuid "6ab44418-4fde-5e9e-8a7f-72d3ba9e3f25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423393} {:answer/id #uuid "9c5f2113-e26c-58e1-947e-c0300530087b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi \\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423398} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b91439-9a71-53df-96c8-314232b56478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423425} {:answer/id #uuid "f82b5eab-7255-5b1f-b41e-fa0214204617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423426}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7b91439-9a71-53df-96c8-314232b56478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423425} :answer-field/id #uuid "017293d9-a2de-5bf2-b2b9-63c72dc5e9c4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c6c92f89-f1d7-5cd5-9f3c-fab3133854ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-161239" :question/problem "![](images/e7/e73d17583a6aa15601abd957f25cfb11ded37159649a67c5b237915d03ed98f7.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x-3z,\\,y,\\,z+3x⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $2$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{2}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=4$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x-3z,\\,y,\\,z+3x⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}⟨x-3z,\\,y,\\,z+3x⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(x(x-3z)+{y}^{2}+z(z+3z)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-3xz+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}+3xz) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}2\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =32\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4a77d700-dee1-58de-add2-b15b7a553b7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423383} {:answer/id #uuid "4b538de2-0bf9-52bf-9303-9311b62ebd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423384} {:answer/id #uuid "8dd5c682-fccc-58c8-80e3-11d1729f0473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423396} {:answer/id #uuid "c4df7c45-b2ce-53b1-a841-5b246b8afa77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423409} {:answer/id #uuid "d97a866d-566f-5022-aaa9-aa18fc519026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b538de2-0bf9-52bf-9303-9311b62ebd0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423384} :answer-field/id #uuid "a4c323a0-5a8f-5466-a1d8-57f7d5162590" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423437}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cea56a0f-1b32-5284-84a0-25dd8320e070" :question/math-academy-id "q-161240" :question/problem "![](images/e6/e6c13f4405c407123973c370a20c88b9c989bd54ade909b06414eabc22f0d45e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨3x+4y,\\,3y-4x,\\,3z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $1$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}=1$ on $S,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨3x+4y,\\,3y-4x,\\,3z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =x(3x+4y)+y(3y-4x)+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}+4xy+3{y}^{2}-4xy+3{z}^{2} \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}3\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3{∬}_{S}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\text{Area}(S) \\\\  & =3\\cdot 4\\pi \\cdot {1}^{2} \\\\  & =12\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278441 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198391 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "315e5799-4bcc-5f2d-b30b-6a3c945e1589" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "253bc184-740c-51c5-bffa-f791ca607ec2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275814} {:answer/id #uuid "3c9bb457-d829-5a5d-9a7b-819fd1e87d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276007} {:answer/id #uuid "90694ad8-9294-5db9-9c43-98c9fb606cc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi \\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276785} {:answer/id #uuid "b7ffdca8-7e8f-5108-9f3c-ca4ffa31d862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277110} {:answer/id #uuid "cacec28a-9f0f-573f-b6ab-d2a206ad0d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277278}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c9bb457-d829-5a5d-9a7b-819fd1e87d0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276007} :answer-field/id #uuid "8284499e-ae11-52db-8d44-1ea843ffae18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8f1bee6-220a-53d7-9c95-ba6fb2fa5c32" :question/math-academy-id "q-161241" :question/problem "![](images/6b/6bd0ac15180df7a413691a1f1f15980b47e88c63e1d3af914175231c172ca97c.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩$ and the boundary $S$ of a spherical solid of radius $3$ centered at the origin. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nFor a sphere or radius $r$ centered at $O,$ the unit normal to the sphere is given by\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{⟨x,y,z⟩}{r}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $r=3.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nn=\\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot n & =⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}⟨x{z}^{2},\\,0,\\,-{x}^{2}z⟩\\cdot ⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}^{2}{z}^{2}+0-{x}^{2}{z}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across $S$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{S}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281493 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "420cd294-1e8e-5212-a308-d90eecc5d95e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279181} {:answer/id #uuid "5092cf90-6737-5481-97b6-b1c9d2d8d151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279328} {:answer/id #uuid "5d671d08-593e-54ef-a9a0-97cfad44a4c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279449} {:answer/id #uuid "a861a6d7-3adf-51df-a023-a9ebbe2085d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "162\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280079} {:answer/id #uuid "cd33a0a4-ed3e-57fb-932a-687f9e5394e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "244\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd33a0a4-ed3e-57fb-932a-687f9e5394e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "244\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280399} :answer-field/id #uuid "34aba361-ce09-5317-89f1-4bb471611ea4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9dd906e8-cb51-59ae-a963-a23ed28e4195" :question/math-academy-id "q-162543" :question/problem "![](images/7c/7ca9996f6d0185d99752ccf5201b5300e0e5149ad674bb9676cf8c716b71a171.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,2y\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $3$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,2y\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](images/32/320e0c3333aa59f4e856d7d869d6fece09b178ac1abb3acd402621977df9b496.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=3$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=3$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,2y\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2(3)\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}6\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =6{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & =6{∬}_{D}\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =6{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}r\\sqrt{25-{r}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =6\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[-\\frac{1}{3}{(25-{r}^{2})}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-4\\pi (64-125) \\\\  & =-4\\pi (-61) \\\\  & =244\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,2(0)\\sqrt{25-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}},\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =244\\pi +0 \\\\  & =244\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e9f6b3b-013a-5d23-ba87-538ff7611718" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278878} {:answer/id #uuid "7214f6ce-83f0-5443-84fc-20acb98afa84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279629} {:answer/id #uuid "7732cf67-03f6-59f5-9b25-da6ee7ccf96a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "243\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279655} {:answer/id #uuid "a3368fe0-436e-54bb-8a95-53b442357715" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280041} {:answer/id #uuid "b9becb59-4c7c-59d1-81e0-eb9920f32f78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "128\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280226}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7214f6ce-83f0-5443-84fc-20acb98afa84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "216\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279629} :answer-field/id #uuid "a60449cf-9d8b-5e61-bc2d-a468d2e83811" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281122}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6aee075a-fd15-5236-b83e-02176157c22a" :question/math-academy-id "q-162547" :question/problem "![](images/de/dedd58b5eeeba8b2874c740543132026f95157597dd12ad31fbaa476a3d77c2e.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $4$ whose base is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](images/9f/9ffcdd701a8ac6b12cd62773e1cabfd2ce95f9a809e18d087c4bf4f4a22eadb5.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=4$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=4$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,{y}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,({4}^{2})(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}16(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =16{∬}_{D}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{∬}_{D}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =16{∬}_{D}(6-{r}^{2})\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}r(6-{r}^{2})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3}(6r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =16{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{3}(6r-{r}^{3})\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[3{r}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =32\\pi (27-\\frac{81}{4}) \\\\  & =32\\pi (\\frac{27}{4}) \\\\  & =216\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 9.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,{0}^{2}(6-{x}^{2}-{z}^{2}),\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =216\\pi +0 \\\\  & =216\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198404 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d07bcae4-cae8-5eb0-a93f-798ba20485a6" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Cylinder"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1cea322b-b0db-5f0b-8d30-fb81863536c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278859} {:answer/id #uuid "4015773d-eaff-56b4-bd3c-b474473744d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279156} {:answer/id #uuid "4291441b-d17e-5a42-84df-e9573611fc4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279191} {:answer/id #uuid "be832e94-e016-59db-9c97-3dc0d4323a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280267} {:answer/id #uuid "e35426ce-4910-5852-8657-998eaac427bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280597}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be832e94-e016-59db-9c97-3dc0d4323a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280267} :answer-field/id #uuid "db5c5640-bf67-5d54-8096-feeae76158b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281215}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11b39dd0-c5da-564f-835c-b92be8cee48e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162550" :question/problem "![](images/fa/fa7f40460a8834c73c61575aef821157e1aff7dace359752fd98593271812c10.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of a right circular cylinder of height $2$ whose base is a circle of radius $1$ centered at the origin, as shown above. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral side of $S$ is parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the left and right sides. \n\n![](images/1f/1f00f0e9fa8148ca3cf161322c926ea9fc16a4467991063cbdcb4f293254674d.png)\n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,y({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}{S}_{1} & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}2({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =2{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying a change of variables $(x,z)\\to (r,\\theta ),\\text{d}A=r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}A & =2{∬}_{D}({r}^{2}+4)\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}r({r}^{2}+4)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{3}+4r)\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}({r}^{3}+4r)\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2\\pi \\cdot {[\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}+2{r}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =4\\pi (\\frac{1}{4}+2) \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for ${x}^{2}+{z}^{2}\\le 1.$ Therefore, for everywhere on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\nF(x,0,z)=⟨0,\\,0({x}^{2}+{z}^{2}+4),\\,0⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}0\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =9\\pi +0 \\\\  & =9\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281497 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4733bd38-68d3-5984-a69e-ff8b9f04c031" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279228} {:answer/id #uuid "4a692b37-af63-53e6-aa36-a3d49a4fc6cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279256} {:answer/id #uuid "7d9be15d-b474-5937-bf5f-ead793ba493a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279718} {:answer/id #uuid "e4525407-bcbe-5e9e-b5a7-773cf80e47be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280606} {:answer/id #uuid "e622ef96-4b2d-5887-a496-919a420b3b2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4525407-bcbe-5e9e-b5a7-773cf80e47be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280606} :answer-field/id #uuid "851949dc-2d6a-5c0b-8037-54c4134fd0a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5ad458af-f12d-5b37-8e06-6096fcdae441" :question/math-academy-id "q-162560" :question/problem "![](images/04/04f58e972500033523e3ca3247ea4e1c5c377d8dc2b402b1c0200d9c85529894.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $y$-component of $F=⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $y$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the right and left sides. \n\n![](images/86/8652a197f603c517cd462c69473e3a693ba8dcd76340b6241f890663356e00cb.png)\n\n \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the right surface. On this surface, we have $y=2$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=2$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,6{x}^{2}+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =6{x}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}6{x}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[2{x}^{3}+2xz]}_{x=0}^{x=2}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}16+4z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[16z+2{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the left surface. On this surface, we have $y=0$ for $0\\le x\\le 2$ and $0\\le z\\le 1.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $y=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨0,\\,3{x}^{2}y+2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2z,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-2{∬}_{D}z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}z\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x{\\int }_{0}^{1}z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-2\\cdot [x]_{0}^{2}\\cdot {[\\frac{{z}^{2}}{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot 2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =18+(-2) \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198390 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "500d2422-474b-55a3-8fe7-05799ba578af" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Flux of a Vector Field Over a Rectangular Solid"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b76772c-2dfc-5597-b55b-022f7c8fe7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279267} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5cb616-4d6b-57dc-a0cf-cd15973a462d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280103} {:answer/id #uuid "bc56d3ac-de6d-5094-8121-3d89792b8766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280246} {:answer/id #uuid "de5d4f12-0e8f-5150-828a-2de6255c782f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280554} {:answer/id #uuid "e4736dcc-7b75-5021-8512-443933abe1f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "de5d4f12-0e8f-5150-828a-2de6255c782f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280554} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71ef8c6-7ff1-5162-aa30-762dd2ff7e93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bdcab013-1b4f-5535-a84d-7952ce7d745b" :question/math-academy-id "q-162563" :question/problem "![](images/63/638a02dec15c2cecd8563854c2fb071ff70f10004f60339ef92d0914e7198ebc.png)\n\nConsider the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩$ and the surface $S$ defined by the bounding region of the rectangular solid shown above in the first octant. Calculate the flux of $F$ through $S.$" :question/worked-solution "If $S$ is an oriented surface with unit normal vector $n,$ and $F$ is continuous vector field, then the flux of $F$ across $S$ measured with respect to $n$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S.\n$$\n\nNotice that only the $x$-component of $F=⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩$ is non-zero. Since the lateral sides of $S$ are parallel to the $x$-axis, the flux of $F$ through $S$ will be non-zero only at the front and back sides. \n\n![](images/4f/4fd228a9bf0a2d8382139d3f804ee7c9b3fd8721c8ad079de43e61b9e8947240.png)\n\n  \n\nLet's consider the flux of $F$ through these two sides.\n\n- Let ${S}_{1}$ denote the front surface. On this surface, we have $x=1$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{1}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{1}=⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=1$ on ${S}_{1},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{1} & =⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =4+3{y}^{2}+2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{1}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}4+3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{[(4+2z)y+{y}^{3}]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}4+2z+1\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}2z+5\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[{z}^{2}+5z]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =9+15 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Let ${S}_{2}$ denote the back surface. On this surface, we have $x=0$ for $0\\le y\\le 1$ and $0\\le z\\le 3.$ The outward-pointing unit normal vector to ${S}_{2}$ is\n\n$$\n{n}_{2}=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩.\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that $x=0$ on ${S}_{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F\\cdot {n}_{2} & =⟨4{x}^{3}+3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{y}^{2}+2z,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,\\,0,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =-(3{y}^{2}+2z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate the flux of $F$ across ${S}_{2}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{2}}-(3{y}^{2}+2z)\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =-{∬}_{D}3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{y}^{2}+2z\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}{[{y}^{3}+2yz]}_{y=0}^{y=1}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{\\int }_{0}^{3}1+2z\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =-{[z+{z}^{2}]}_{0}^{3} \\\\  & =-(3+9) \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & ={∬}_{{S}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S+{∬}_{{S}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S \\\\  & =24+(-12) \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
